#include "../include/head.hh"

<body>
		#include "../include/header.hh"
		#include "../include/elec_header.hh"

		MARK_ACTIVE("erc")

	<link rel="stylesheet" href="/e88.css">
		<link rel="stylesheet" href="/index.css">

		<article id="eerc_stuff">
			<h1>Turbo Modding RC Cars</h1>	
			<h2>History</h2>
			<div class="art_content">
				<h3>Motivation</h3>
				<p>I bought some cheap rc-cars from the interwebs, out of curiosity. They work pretty well but the horsepower leaves a lot to be desired.</p>
				<p>Then, I remembered that Tamiya sells replacement motors for their model cars, which happened to be compatible.
				
				<h3>Building process</h3>
				<p>I started by replacing the motors. However, the original control board was unable to handle the increase current, and kept cutting out. This means that a new higher-power circuit is needed to truly unlock the potential of the motor.</p>
				<p>I opted for a fully dedicated MOSFET H-bridge circuit. A bit of soldering and connections later and there is a clear improvement in horsepower, allowing the car to burnout its wheels and accelerate incredibly quickly.</p>
				<img src="/projects/turborc.jxl"  alt="Mod internals" style="max-width: 100%">
				<p>To enable more fine-grained control of the throttle, I replaced the original control board with an ESP32-C3.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="art_content">
			

			<h3>Software development process</h3>
			
				<p>
				I developed a simple control program for the ESP32-c3 using platformio, that receives controller input from an Android app. Using an Android app meant that I could use a large variety of joysticks to control the car.
				</p>
				<p>
				The latency and unreliability of Bluetooth Low Power meant that many hacks were used in order to keep the controls as real-time as possible.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="art_content">
			

	</div>

	</div>


</body>
